{"chunk_id": "649c568d2760", "text": "After hearing the continuing lament for the dead of the Kurukshetra war, grief spreads again through the Kuru household. Gandhari appeals to the sage Vyasa, describing how Dhritarashtra, the widows of the slain princes, Kunti, Draupadi, and Subhadra all remain tormented years after the slaughter. She begs the powerful ascetic to grant them relief by allowing them to see their departed loved ones. Prompted by Vyasa, Kunti then confesses a long\u2011hidden secret: in her youth the sage Durvasas gave her a mantra to summon gods. Out of curiosity she invoked the Sun god, Surya, who granted her a son\u2014Karna. Fearing dishonor, she abandoned the child in a river and later kept silent even after recognizing him. Vyasa consoles her, declaring she bears no sin and promising that her wish to behold her son will be fulfilled.\nGandhari recounts the families\u2019 grief after the war, and Kunti confesses to Vyasa the secret birth and abandonment of her son Karna.\nThemes: maternal grief, confession and guilt, aftermath of war, divine conception, longing for the dead", "vector": [0.010991225950419903, -0.021995987743139267, 0.0006501506431959569, -0.002680126577615738, 9.025757753988728e-05, 0.02326837182044983, -0.014036823995411396, 0.0032841702923178673, -0.03394826874136925, -0.0235932357609272, 0.021562835201621056, 0.013461544178426266, 0.022564498707652092, 0.017840437591075897, -0.014266936108469963, 0.010551306419074535, 0.039958249777555466, -0.008649499155580997, 0.040364328771829605, -0.034246060997247696, -0.010747577995061874, -0.002801950555294752, -0.011464986018836498, 0.013353256508708, -0.02709905616939068, 0.022293779999017715, 0.013468312099575996, -0.01859845407307148, 0.00956317875534296, -0.026259824633598328, -0.026110930368304253, 0.008006539195775986, 0.003928821999579668, -0.01828712597489357, -0.015363351441919804, 0.009698539040982723, 0.0015498711727559566, -0.02463550679385662, 0.0070657879114151, 0.0034601